1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:06,157
This is the strain in Titan's hull on
dive eighty, the fifteenth of July, 2022.

2
00:00:06,772 --> 00:00:10,719
That afternoon, the four people
inside heard a loud bang.

3
00:00:11,087 --> 00:00:12,521
And the line jumped.

4
00:00:13,133 --> 00:00:17,403
OceanGate's director of engineering
later testified that Stockton Rush, the

5
00:00:17,443 --> 00:00:20,189
chief executive, wanted to keep diving.

6
00:00:20,453 --> 00:00:24,153
[Phil Brooks] I think the
plan was at that time to

7
00:00:24,193 --> 00:00:27,933
"well, let's dive again
and see what happens" on 81

8
00:00:28,748 --> 00:00:31,589
Titan dived twice more to the Titanic.

9
00:00:32,206 --> 00:00:38,275
Eleven months later, on its first deep
dive of 2023, the hull failed, and all

10
00:00:38,315 --> 00:00:40,277
five people on board died.

11
00:00:40,997 --> 00:00:44,335
It is often said the warning
worked, and was ignored.

12
00:00:44,927 --> 00:00:47,009
The record is more complicated.

13
00:00:47,724 --> 00:00:52,183
OceanGate plotted this strain, and
the depth beside it, against time.

14
00:00:52,722 --> 00:00:58,154
After the implosion, the NTSB, America's
accident investigator, plotted the

15
00:00:58,194 --> 00:00:59,978
same numbers against depth.

16
00:01:00,475 --> 00:01:04,423
We will draw that chart from their
numbers, and ask why a company that read

17
00:01:04,463 --> 00:01:08,710
this data after dive eighty did
not know its hull was damaged.

18
00:01:09,261 --> 00:01:11,732
The eighteenth of June, 2023.

19
00:01:12,104 --> 00:01:16,138
At about nine in the morning, Titan
was released from its launch platform.

20
00:01:16,499 --> 00:01:20,770
It was the first dive of that year's
expedition to go deeper than 10 metres.

21
00:01:21,171 --> 00:01:27,624
At 10:47, at about 3,350 metres,
Titan sent a routine message:

22
00:01:27,785 --> 00:01:32,274
it was dropping two weights, a standard
step at that depth to slow the descent.

23
00:01:32,605 --> 00:01:34,936
It was the last message Titan sent.

24
00:01:35,340 --> 00:01:38,647
A minute later, the support ship
acknowledged it, and kept sending

25
00:01:38,687 --> 00:01:40,090
messages for hours.

26
00:01:40,494 --> 00:01:41,783
No reply came.

27
00:01:42,262 --> 00:01:46,534
Four days later, a remotely operated
vehicle found the tail cone and both

28
00:01:46,574 --> 00:01:51,145
titanium domes on the sea floor, a few
hundred metres from the Titanic's bow.

29
00:01:51,506 --> 00:01:58,603
By its dive log, Titan had reached the
wreck, 3,840 metres down, thirteen times.

30
00:01:58,964 --> 00:02:02,963
On its last dive, it failed
about 500 metres shallower.

31
00:02:03,299 --> 00:02:05,654
So where did the margin of error run out?

32
00:02:06,266 --> 00:02:08,157
And when did the hull start to go?

33
00:02:12,231 --> 00:02:17,836
At 3,840 metres, the ocean presses on
every surface with about three hundred

34
00:02:17,876 --> 00:02:20,358
and eighty times the
pressure at the surface.

35
00:02:20,648 --> 00:02:23,963
Titan was not built the way
deep submersibles usually are.

36
00:02:24,169 --> 00:02:28,785
Its pressure hull was a cylinder of
carbon fibre, five layers, each about an

37
00:02:28,825 --> 00:02:31,473
inch thick, bonded one onto the next.

38
00:02:31,698 --> 00:02:35,584
Titanium rings were bonded to
each end, and titanium domes were

39
00:02:35,624 --> 00:02:36,965
bolted to the rings.

40
00:02:37,327 --> 00:02:40,916
According to a former director of
marine operations, the company's chief

41
00:02:40,956 --> 00:02:45,106
executive chose carbon fibre because he
believed it was better in compression

42
00:02:45,146 --> 00:02:46,208
than in tension.

43
00:02:46,533 --> 00:02:50,542
He also believed that, because it was
lighter, it would need less buoyancy,

44
00:02:50,642 --> 00:02:54,411
could be launched from many more kinds
of ship instead of a purpose-built one,

45
00:02:54,451 --> 00:02:55,918
and could carry more people.

46
00:02:55,958 --> 00:02:57,394
How strong was it?

47
00:02:57,616 --> 00:03:02,541
The company's original goal was a hull
rated to 6,000 metres, with a safety

48
00:03:02,581 --> 00:03:08,027
factor of 2.25: a hull that should not
fail until about thirteen and a half

49
00:03:08,067 --> 00:03:09,128
thousand metres.

50
00:03:09,531 --> 00:03:13,867
Canada's Transportation Safety Board
found that OceanGate later changed that

51
00:03:13,907 --> 00:03:16,635
safety factor to 1.25.

52
00:03:16,835 --> 00:03:20,485
The analysis for this hull said the
carbon cylinder would hold to about

53
00:03:20,525 --> 00:03:25,838
7,500 metres: six thousand, times 1.25.

54
00:03:26,082 --> 00:03:28,630
Less than twice the depth of the Titanic.

55
00:03:28,816 --> 00:03:34,409
And, the NTSB noted, that analysis
assumed perfect geometry and perfectly

56
00:03:34,449 --> 00:03:37,896
aligned fibres, not the hull
that was actually built.

57
00:03:38,197 --> 00:03:42,731
Scale models built with the new
materials imploded, or nearly imploded,

58
00:03:42,792 --> 00:03:44,959
shallower than 3,000 metres.

59
00:03:45,199 --> 00:03:49,148
OceanGate traced those failures to
wrinkles in the carbon, and changed its

60
00:03:49,188 --> 00:03:50,490
method to prevent them.

61
00:03:50,642 --> 00:03:54,557
That new method, bonding the
layers one onto the next, was never

62
00:03:54,597 --> 00:03:55,841
tested on a model.

63
00:03:56,010 --> 00:04:00,343
Canada's investigators found that a
scale model built that way was discussed,

64
00:04:00,383 --> 00:04:03,071
and was not built, in order to save money.

65
00:04:03,268 --> 00:04:06,681
The only one ever built was
the hull that carried people.

66
00:04:06,954 --> 00:04:11,772
In early 2021, that hull went to a
pressure test facility in Maryland.

67
00:04:11,867 --> 00:04:16,396
The test plan had it going to the
pressure of 4,200 metres on three of

68
00:04:16,436 --> 00:04:17,538
the four days.

69
00:04:17,687 --> 00:04:21,062
The facility's logbook shows
it reached that pressure once.

70
00:04:21,201 --> 00:04:23,750
The first of March, for twenty minutes.

71
00:04:23,986 --> 00:04:27,859
According to analysis done for the
company, the test depth was limited by a

72
00:04:27,899 --> 00:04:30,627
titanium dome, not by the carbon.

73
00:04:30,885 --> 00:04:35,536
On the other days, the hull was held
near 4,000 metres, and then at three

74
00:04:35,576 --> 00:04:39,223
thousand eight hundred and
forty, for four hours, twice.

75
00:04:39,382 --> 00:04:41,440
It was never tested to failure.

76
00:04:42,833 --> 00:04:47,156
The NTSB found it unlikely that the
hull ever had the strength the analysis

77
00:04:47,196 --> 00:04:51,038
predicted, and that the company
did not know its actual strength.

78
00:04:51,214 --> 00:04:53,587
Then how would anyone
know if it was failing?

79
00:04:53,830 --> 00:04:56,448
OceanGate's answer was to listen to it.

80
00:04:56,636 --> 00:05:01,494
Eight acoustic sensors and sixteen
strain gauges, fixed inside the hull.

81
00:05:01,695 --> 00:05:05,926
A carbon hull, the theory went, would
crackle as it weakened, and the company

82
00:05:05,966 --> 00:05:07,610
would retire it in time.

83
00:05:07,717 --> 00:05:09,294
The system had limits.

84
00:05:09,436 --> 00:05:14,701
The pilot saw live readings, but the full
data were downloaded only after each dive.

85
00:05:14,873 --> 00:05:19,038
Acoustic data were analysed only from
the dive itself, so damage at the

86
00:05:19,078 --> 00:05:22,422
surface, or in storage,
could not raise an alert.

87
00:05:22,594 --> 00:05:27,169
In 2022, three of the eight acoustic
channels registered no acoustic

88
00:05:27,209 --> 00:05:28,729
events all season.

89
00:05:28,769 --> 00:05:32,920
After each dive, the engineering
director looked at the data first, and

90
00:05:32,960 --> 00:05:37,070
emailed it to about eight to ten
people, the chief executive among them.

91
00:05:37,156 --> 00:05:41,032
And the alert criteria had been set on
the sounds of earlier hulls and scale

92
00:05:41,072 --> 00:05:42,819
models, built a different way.

93
00:05:43,082 --> 00:05:48,611
No model had ever been built this hull's
way, so, the NTSB found, OceanGate

94
00:05:48,651 --> 00:05:51,976
had no data on how this one
would sound as it failed.

95
00:05:55,528 --> 00:05:57,289
Had anyone warned about this?

96
00:05:57,869 --> 00:06:00,360
The eighteenth of January, 2018.

97
00:06:00,630 --> 00:06:04,595
The company's director of marine
operations filed an inspection report on

98
00:06:04,635 --> 00:06:06,037
Titan's first hull.

99
00:06:06,164 --> 00:06:08,935
It listed twenty-six items needing action.

100
00:06:09,148 --> 00:06:12,772
On the hull, he wrote that the cut-off
ends of the cylinder showed very

101
00:06:12,812 --> 00:06:16,956
visible signs of delamination, and
he warned of potential inter-laminar

102
00:06:16,996 --> 00:06:19,058
fatigue due to pressure cycling.

103
00:06:19,245 --> 00:06:23,719
The next day, he met the chief executive
and argued for more testing of the hull.

104
00:06:23,889 --> 00:06:26,090
The chief executive recorded the meeting.

105
00:06:26,323 --> 00:06:30,511
On that recording, he answered that the
cut-off ends were scrap, cut away for

106
00:06:30,551 --> 00:06:35,019
that reason, and that acoustic monitoring
would tell him way before the hull failed.

107
00:06:35,274 --> 00:06:37,851
Four days later, the director was fired.

108
00:06:38,036 --> 00:06:41,793
The termination letter said the two of
them were at an impasse over the hull.

109
00:06:42,094 --> 00:06:46,321
In March, more than twenty submersible
professionals signed a letter urging

110
00:06:46,361 --> 00:06:50,168
the chief executive to put safety
first, and to follow the industry's

111
00:06:50,208 --> 00:06:51,690
established practices.

112
00:06:51,928 --> 00:06:53,484
It was never sent formally.

113
00:06:53,702 --> 00:06:57,433
He received it, called the head of the
industry committee behind it, and said

114
00:06:57,473 --> 00:06:59,258
he was leaving the association.

115
00:06:59,727 --> 00:07:01,262
That was the first hull.

116
00:07:01,403 --> 00:07:05,190
But the failure mode named in that
report, delamination, is what the

117
00:07:05,230 --> 00:07:07,453
investigators would find in the second.

118
00:07:07,712 --> 00:07:11,343
In 2019, a crack was
found in the first hull.

119
00:07:11,563 --> 00:07:15,588
The company's first director of engineering
testified that he refused to sign

120
00:07:15,628 --> 00:07:19,312
it off, and that his refusal kept
it from that year's expedition.

121
00:07:19,511 --> 00:07:22,854
He said it angered the chief
executive, and led to his firing.

122
00:07:22,941 --> 00:07:26,172
The company's letter to him
cited the cancelled expedition.

123
00:07:26,567 --> 00:07:30,126
A second hull was built:
the one lost in 2023.

124
00:07:30,431 --> 00:07:33,128
Its dives were numbered
fifty to eighty-eight.

125
00:07:34,945 --> 00:07:36,593
Now, dive eighty.

126
00:07:37,039 --> 00:07:42,276
On the fifteenth of July, 2022, Titan
reached the Titanic and came back up.

127
00:07:42,548 --> 00:07:45,994
Near the surface, the people
inside heard a loud bang.

128
00:07:46,176 --> 00:07:48,408
So did the crew of a small boat above.

129
00:07:48,534 --> 00:07:51,924
The records differ on exactly
where it happened, but all place it

130
00:07:51,964 --> 00:07:52,986
near the surface.

131
00:07:53,224 --> 00:07:58,150
On channel two, the acoustic sensor
peaked above 25,000 millivolts.

132
00:07:58,264 --> 00:08:01,512
At the same moment, the strain
gauges beside it jumped.

133
00:08:01,552 --> 00:08:05,539
Phil Brooks was the director of
engineering by then, promoted from software

134
00:08:05,579 --> 00:08:07,262
engineer before that season.

135
00:08:07,697 --> 00:08:12,307
He testified that he had no background
in mechanical engineering or materials,

136
00:08:12,368 --> 00:08:16,076
and that the agreement was that others,
mainly the chief executive, would

137
00:08:16,116 --> 00:08:17,519
handle those tasks.

138
00:08:17,948 --> 00:08:21,202
Under oath, he described
what they did after the bang.

139
00:08:21,575 --> 00:08:23,861
They inspected the hull
as best they could.

140
00:08:24,081 --> 00:08:28,039
But the inside was lined by an
insert, and the outside was covered.

141
00:08:28,249 --> 00:08:32,056
You can take a flashlight, he said,
and look down a small gap between the

142
00:08:32,096 --> 00:08:33,278
insert and the hull.

143
00:08:33,678 --> 00:08:37,528
[Phil Brooks] But it's not —
you know, it's not a great test

144
00:08:38,204 --> 00:08:40,736
The insert, he said, was never removed.

145
00:08:40,776 --> 00:08:41,943
That was never done.

146
00:08:42,241 --> 00:08:44,150
So the main check was the data.

147
00:08:44,406 --> 00:08:48,522
The strain showed a shift in level,
on two of the sixteen channels.

148
00:08:48,738 --> 00:08:52,741
A sudden shift, he said, is something
to be concerned about, for sure.

149
00:08:52,930 --> 00:08:57,252
He added that he was not a strain expert,
and that this one looked fairly minor.

150
00:08:57,613 --> 00:09:02,343
[Phil Brooks] Q. So who made the
decision to continue to resume

151
00:09:02,383 --> 00:09:07,153
diving operations after the report of
the loud bang? A. Definitely Stockton.

152
00:09:08,953 --> 00:09:13,656
[Phil Brooks] I know I discussed it
with Stockton and he — you know, he

153
00:09:13,696 --> 00:09:18,400
wanted to continue on, that I think
the plan was at that time to "well,

154
00:09:18,440 --> 00:09:23,183
let's dive again and see what
happens" on 81, on the next dives

155
00:09:23,963 --> 00:09:25,828
What did they think the bang was?

156
00:09:26,180 --> 00:09:30,062
Brooks testified that the chief
executive's theory was the frame.

157
00:09:30,241 --> 00:09:34,248
The hull compresses slightly on the way
down and the frame bolted to it does

158
00:09:34,288 --> 00:09:39,015
not, so on the way up the frame may have
sprung, and snapped back into shape.

159
00:09:39,211 --> 00:09:43,065
Brooks said that, without being a
qualified mechanical engineer, he found it

160
00:09:43,105 --> 00:09:44,489
plausible at the time.

161
00:09:44,865 --> 00:09:48,672
A contractor on that expedition testified
that she asked him whether the data

162
00:09:48,712 --> 00:09:50,235
could show delamination.

163
00:09:50,525 --> 00:09:54,633
In her account, he said it was only a
few microns, and that the next two dives

164
00:09:54,673 --> 00:09:57,938
would go ahead, with a visual
inspection back in Seattle.

165
00:09:58,125 --> 00:10:01,911
Brooks himself testified that they had
converted the shift into a physical

166
00:10:01,951 --> 00:10:05,296
distance, and judged it a
relatively small movement.

167
00:10:05,746 --> 00:10:08,614
Titan dived again on
the nineteenth of July.

168
00:10:09,024 --> 00:10:13,371
Brooks testified that after that dive,
they didn't see any further shifts

169
00:10:13,411 --> 00:10:14,573
in strain data.

170
00:10:14,788 --> 00:10:16,083
No loud bangs.

171
00:10:16,131 --> 00:10:18,976
Nothing in the acoustics that
seemed out of the ordinary.

172
00:10:19,635 --> 00:10:23,744
One acoustic sensor, he testified,
did show activity on the dives that

173
00:10:23,784 --> 00:10:28,255
followed that they had not seen in 2021,
the kind of thing they wanted to be

174
00:10:28,295 --> 00:10:29,457
concerned about.

175
00:10:29,694 --> 00:10:31,705
He raised it with the chief executive.

176
00:10:31,929 --> 00:10:36,460
It stayed under the threshold they had
set, he said, or maybe just barely over

177
00:10:36,500 --> 00:10:40,308
it, and it never got worse, so he
did not think it was a concern.

178
00:10:40,617 --> 00:10:44,714
The NTSB found the sensors never produced
the kind of signal that would have

179
00:10:44,754 --> 00:10:45,978
triggered an alert.

180
00:10:46,286 --> 00:10:48,338
So, was the damage in the data?

181
00:10:48,825 --> 00:10:53,375
[Phil Brooks] So what I
looked for in strain data was

182
00:10:53,415 --> 00:10:57,965
the linearity with depth, that
if it went nonlinear with the

183
00:10:58,005 --> 00:11:02,595
depth profile, that that was
something to be concerned about.

184
00:11:03,395 --> 00:11:06,430
[Dr. Donald Kramer] OceanGate's
strain data was typically and

185
00:11:06,470 --> 00:11:09,545
perhaps always plotted
as a function of time.

186
00:11:10,251 --> 00:11:14,507
The depth was there, on the same
chart as the strain, but both ran

187
00:11:14,547 --> 00:11:15,650
against the clock.

188
00:11:15,965 --> 00:11:20,100
Every dive has its own shape,
its own pace, its own stops.

189
00:11:20,272 --> 00:11:23,341
Laid side by side, the
dives are hard to compare.

190
00:11:23,708 --> 00:11:28,017
The NTSB said a more appropriate
way was to plot strain against depth

191
00:11:28,057 --> 00:11:30,923
directly, because depth sets the pressure.

192
00:11:31,210 --> 00:11:34,019
So here is the same data, against depth.

193
00:11:35,636 --> 00:11:38,154
Dive seventy-five, a straight line.

194
00:11:38,282 --> 00:11:42,834
Dive eighty, the same straight line,
until the event at its very end.

195
00:11:42,874 --> 00:11:44,101
Dive eighty-one.

196
00:11:44,220 --> 00:11:45,397
Dive eighty-two.

197
00:11:45,528 --> 00:11:46,812
Dive eighty-three.

198
00:11:46,989 --> 00:11:50,743
In the first 500 metres,
every one of them bends away.

199
00:11:51,012 --> 00:11:55,744
To put a number on it, the NTSB fitted
a line to the shallowest stretch, from

200
00:11:55,784 --> 00:11:58,069
fifteen to a hundred and fifty metres:

201
00:11:58,130 --> 00:12:01,036
metres of depth for each
millivolt from the gauge.

202
00:12:01,146 --> 00:12:05,572
Before the event: 5.00, and 4.74.

203
00:12:05,833 --> 00:12:11,173
After it: 8.76, 8.09, 8.77.

204
00:12:11,459 --> 00:12:14,325
Same gauge, same hull, same sea.

205
00:12:15,278 --> 00:12:18,314
A response that had changed
by about three quarters.

206
00:12:18,532 --> 00:12:20,849
Could OceanGate have
seen this at the time?

207
00:12:21,297 --> 00:12:25,285
The Coast Guard board found that OceanGate
had all the data it needed to show

208
00:12:25,325 --> 00:12:29,313
the hull's behaviour had changed, and
that analysing strain against elapsed

209
00:12:29,353 --> 00:12:31,196
time failed to reveal it.

210
00:12:31,375 --> 00:12:34,992
The timing that follows is our own
reading of the record, not a finding

211
00:12:35,032 --> 00:12:36,217
of any report.

212
00:12:36,423 --> 00:12:38,913
Brooks was right that
there was no new jump.

213
00:12:39,056 --> 00:12:44,337
What changed was the slope, and against
time a change in slope is hard to see.

214
00:12:44,563 --> 00:12:48,075
Dive eighty itself shows
nothing until its last minutes.

215
00:12:48,169 --> 00:12:53,284
In the NTSB's plots, the bend
first appears in dive eighty-one.

216
00:12:53,519 --> 00:12:56,862
That data was on the company's
computer after that dive.

217
00:12:56,928 --> 00:13:00,982
Plotted against depth, it would have
shown the bend before dive eighty-two,

218
00:13:01,022 --> 00:13:04,051
three days later, and
before dive eighty-three.

219
00:13:04,237 --> 00:13:06,286
And it was not a faulty gauge.

220
00:13:06,545 --> 00:13:11,833
Deeper down, the curves still match,
which the NTSB said shows the gauges

221
00:13:11,873 --> 00:13:13,195
had not changed.

222
00:13:13,328 --> 00:13:14,627
The hull had.

223
00:13:14,978 --> 00:13:19,642
Canada's investigators later plotted
the strain against depth their own way,

224
00:13:19,883 --> 00:13:22,285
measuring each dive against itself.

225
00:13:22,716 --> 00:13:26,980
On the nineteenth of July, for one
gauge at the top of the hull, the slope

226
00:13:27,020 --> 00:13:30,924
from ten to three hundred metres
was nearly double its slope for

227
00:13:30,964 --> 00:13:32,225
the whole dive.

228
00:13:32,434 --> 00:13:37,628
They also found a smaller bend a year
earlier, in August 2021, and could not

229
00:13:37,668 --> 00:13:40,395
determine whether the
bang caused the change.

230
00:13:40,669 --> 00:13:44,234
Why would a damaged hull
bend only near the surface?

231
00:13:44,597 --> 00:13:49,696
The NTSB found long voids in the bond
between the first and second layers, and

232
00:13:49,736 --> 00:13:51,321
between the third and fourth.

233
00:13:51,578 --> 00:13:55,431
Where a layer separates at the end of
the cylinder, it can move on its own

234
00:13:55,471 --> 00:13:56,794
under light pressure.

235
00:13:57,001 --> 00:14:01,456
Deeper down, it found, the layers
respond like the intact hull.

236
00:14:01,712 --> 00:14:04,350
Titan made three more dives that July.

237
00:14:04,515 --> 00:14:09,085
Two of them, eighty-one and eighty-two,
still reached the Titanic, which the

238
00:14:09,125 --> 00:14:13,514
NTSB said showed the first damage
was not enough to make it implode.

239
00:14:13,706 --> 00:14:16,677
A delamination is not a
crack through the wall.

240
00:14:16,991 --> 00:14:19,639
But the wall was no
longer what it had been.

241
00:14:19,966 --> 00:14:25,554
The NTSB's finding: because its analysis
was flawed, OceanGate was unable to

242
00:14:25,594 --> 00:14:29,941
identify that the Titan was damaged
after dive eighty, and that it needed to

243
00:14:29,981 --> 00:14:32,424
be immediately removed from service.

244
00:14:34,170 --> 00:14:36,535
Then it had to spend the winter somewhere.

245
00:14:36,717 --> 00:14:40,951
On the twenty-sixth of July, Titan was
set down in a parking lot at a marine

246
00:14:40,991 --> 00:14:43,177
base in Saint John's, Newfoundland.

247
00:14:43,388 --> 00:14:44,388
Uncovered.

248
00:14:44,491 --> 00:14:48,437
Two days later, the base passed
on a quote for a protective cover:

249
00:14:48,618 --> 00:14:51,662
C$1,750.

250
00:14:51,885 --> 00:14:56,040
The base told the Coast Guard board that
OceanGate never answered that email.

251
00:14:56,250 --> 00:14:58,432
In September, the base wrote again:

252
00:14:58,533 --> 00:15:00,735
Titan still had not been covered.

253
00:15:00,971 --> 00:15:04,393
OceanGate's logistics director
said it would be discussed.

254
00:15:04,546 --> 00:15:06,873
The board found no action followed.

255
00:15:06,990 --> 00:15:10,935
It stayed outside until the sixth of
February, through temperatures from one

256
00:15:10,975 --> 00:15:15,621
point four to eighty-four point two
degrees Fahrenheit, about minus seventeen

257
00:15:15,661 --> 00:15:20,067
to twenty-nine Celsius, through
freezing rain, sleet and snow.

258
00:15:20,243 --> 00:15:23,974
Brooks testified that what they really
wanted was to bring Titan back to

259
00:15:24,014 --> 00:15:27,785
Everett, pull the insert, and
look inside the hull for cracks.

260
00:15:28,102 --> 00:15:32,032
[Phil Brooks] And we were
told it was a cost issue,

261
00:15:32,072 --> 00:15:36,042
you know, that the cost of
shipping it back was prohibitive

262
00:15:36,726 --> 00:15:38,691
Was money behind these decisions?

263
00:15:38,928 --> 00:15:40,949
The Coast Guard board says it was:

264
00:15:41,030 --> 00:15:45,253
in its words, a troubling
prioritization of cost and time-saving

265
00:15:45,293 --> 00:15:46,754
measures over safety.

266
00:15:46,980 --> 00:15:50,128
The NTSB's findings do not address money.

267
00:15:50,306 --> 00:15:56,713
From June 2022 to May 2023, the board
found, the chief executive advanced

268
00:15:56,753 --> 00:15:59,916
the company one point
eight five million dollars.

269
00:16:00,240 --> 00:16:04,594
The board found that employees were asked
to temporarily forgo their salaries,

270
00:16:04,634 --> 00:16:06,259
with a promise of back pay.

271
00:16:06,477 --> 00:16:11,409
The ship that had carried Titan was
unavailable for 2023, the board found.

272
00:16:11,642 --> 00:16:15,977
Titan would be towed to the site on its
launch platform, behind a smaller ship.

273
00:16:16,322 --> 00:16:19,151
Early in 2023, Brooks left.

274
00:16:19,525 --> 00:16:24,173
Under oath, he said his main reason
was that plan to tow Titan, which meant

275
00:16:24,213 --> 00:16:29,001
working on its platform in open seas, and
that the reasons for it were economic.

276
00:16:29,405 --> 00:16:33,220
[Phil Brooks] I felt
that the safety was just

277
00:16:33,260 --> 00:16:37,115
being compromised way too
much. At least for myself.

278
00:16:37,943 --> 00:16:41,952
He said he suggested they not go,
and was told that wasn't possible:

279
00:16:42,053 --> 00:16:43,716
they had people that paid.

280
00:16:43,934 --> 00:16:47,579
The board found that the chief executive
then left the director of engineering

281
00:16:47,619 --> 00:16:53,827
post vacant, in its words, to ensure the
2023 expedition proceeded unhindered.

282
00:16:54,034 --> 00:16:58,963
And the board drew the line back to
2019, when, by his own testimony, the

283
00:16:59,003 --> 00:17:03,090
first director of engineering had kept
a damaged hull from an expedition.

284
00:17:03,383 --> 00:17:07,577
Leaving the post empty, it wrote,
removed the possibility of a replacement

285
00:17:07,617 --> 00:17:09,664
director blocking the expedition.

286
00:17:09,983 --> 00:17:14,656
That spring, at sea and in port,
the platform tipped more than once.

287
00:17:14,952 --> 00:17:20,395
Once, in May, its bow went partly under
water, and Titan's tail cone was lost.

288
00:17:20,661 --> 00:17:23,921
How was a hull like this
allowed to carry passengers?

289
00:17:24,127 --> 00:17:27,840
By the NTSB's reading
of the law, it was not.

290
00:17:28,584 --> 00:17:34,070
Under US law, the NTSB notes, a
submersible carrying even one passenger for

291
00:17:34,110 --> 00:17:38,595
hire is a small passenger vessel, and
must be inspected by the Coast Guard.

292
00:17:38,814 --> 00:17:42,945
OceanGate never registered Titan,
and never arranged an inspection.

293
00:17:43,212 --> 00:17:47,522
The Coast Guard board found that OceanGate
described its paying passengers as

294
00:17:47,562 --> 00:17:51,711
mission specialists, with mandatory
duties, to conceal that they were

295
00:17:51,751 --> 00:17:53,114
paying passengers.

296
00:17:53,317 --> 00:17:56,264
The regulator's own guidance
gets the same scrutiny.

297
00:17:56,509 --> 00:17:59,880
The Coast Guard's voluntary guidance
for passenger submersibles was

298
00:17:59,920 --> 00:18:02,286
published in July 1993.

299
00:18:02,598 --> 00:18:07,466
That December, a new law changed the
definition, to cover any submersible with

300
00:18:07,506 --> 00:18:09,048
one passenger for hire.

301
00:18:09,293 --> 00:18:14,920
As of its report in October 2025, the
NTSB found, the guidance had never been

302
00:18:14,960 --> 00:18:16,302
updated to match.

303
00:18:16,512 --> 00:18:20,639
The Coast Guard, it found, had no
specific inspection standards for how a

304
00:18:20,679 --> 00:18:22,838
submersible is designed or built.

305
00:18:22,878 --> 00:18:27,334
That guidance also recommended that
a submersible dive no deeper than its

306
00:18:27,374 --> 00:18:28,697
rescue could reach.

307
00:18:28,919 --> 00:18:34,158
The NTSB found that Titan regularly
dived to the Titanic with no rescue

308
00:18:34,198 --> 00:18:36,084
standing by that could reach it.

309
00:18:36,279 --> 00:18:40,767
The NTSB found that voluntary guidance
and the rules for small passenger

310
00:18:40,807 --> 00:18:44,474
vessels were insufficient to make
OceanGate follow the industry's

311
00:18:44,514 --> 00:18:46,036
established standards.

312
00:18:50,097 --> 00:18:55,411
In 2023, Titan's first four dives
went no deeper than 10 metres.

313
00:18:55,679 --> 00:18:59,864
At some point after dive eighty-two,
the hull was damaged again.

314
00:19:00,040 --> 00:19:03,712
The NTSB calls that
damage of unknown origin.

315
00:19:03,834 --> 00:19:07,980
The Coast Guard board says the damage
from dive eighty was likely made worse

316
00:19:08,020 --> 00:19:11,425
by the winter outdoors, and
points to the towing as well.

317
00:19:11,671 --> 00:19:13,397
Did every dive weaken it?

318
00:19:13,630 --> 00:19:15,725
Here the investigators differ.

319
00:19:15,925 --> 00:19:22,002
The NTSB dates two events: dive eighty,
and something after dive eighty-two.

320
00:19:22,141 --> 00:19:25,991
Canada's Transportation Safety Board
found the cylinder likely failed

321
00:19:26,031 --> 00:19:29,961
progressively, with damage
accumulating during each dive.

322
00:19:30,152 --> 00:19:34,794
On the eighteenth of June, Titan made
its first deep dive in eleven months.

323
00:19:34,912 --> 00:19:40,004
The NTSB found that the existing
delaminations and the later damage likely led

324
00:19:40,044 --> 00:19:42,008
to a local buckling failure.

325
00:19:42,138 --> 00:19:46,866
A delamination splits one thick layer
into two thinner ones, and thinner

326
00:19:46,906 --> 00:19:48,609
layers buckle more easily.

327
00:19:48,802 --> 00:19:53,197
In a local buckle, one part of the
wall changes shape, and gives way.

328
00:19:53,385 --> 00:19:57,373
The damage from dive eighty, it found,
had reached the forward end of the

329
00:19:57,413 --> 00:20:01,139
cylinder: that is where the
gauge that showed the change sat.

330
00:20:01,349 --> 00:20:05,884
Here is the dive record, from dive
seventy-five to eighty-eight, with each

331
00:20:05,924 --> 00:20:08,592
dated decision beside
the dive it followed.

332
00:20:08,806 --> 00:20:12,901
Six of these dives reached 3,840 metres.

333
00:20:13,065 --> 00:20:14,622
Thirteen did, in all.

334
00:20:14,872 --> 00:20:18,935
Then one line that stops,
about 500 metres short.

335
00:20:21,397 --> 00:20:23,565
Two things often said about Titan.

336
00:20:23,868 --> 00:20:28,312
Its controller: on dive eighty-one,
the thruster controls were found to be

337
00:20:28,352 --> 00:20:32,195
mapped in reverse, and the pilot
turned the controller around.

338
00:20:32,558 --> 00:20:36,015
None of the three reports names
the controller as a cause.

339
00:20:36,478 --> 00:20:41,789
Its window: the Coast Guard board
criticised it as untested and uncertified,

340
00:20:41,830 --> 00:20:45,097
but found it unlikely that the
window caused the implosion.

341
00:20:45,417 --> 00:20:48,124
All three reports place
the failure in the hull.

342
00:20:49,976 --> 00:20:55,023
The NTSB's probable cause was OceanGate's
inadequate engineering process,

343
00:20:55,063 --> 00:20:59,009
which failed to establish the actual
strength and durability of the Titan

344
00:20:59,049 --> 00:21:00,049
pressure vessel.

345
00:21:00,192 --> 00:21:04,158
Contributing were the guidance and the
rules that did not make OceanGate meet

346
00:21:04,198 --> 00:21:08,145
the industry's standards, and the
company's flawed analysis of its own

347
00:21:08,185 --> 00:21:09,326
monitoring data.

348
00:21:09,550 --> 00:21:13,262
The Coast Guard's Marine Board
called it a preventable tragedy.

349
00:21:13,593 --> 00:21:17,980
Canada's Transportation Safety Board
found that Titan received no oversight

350
00:21:18,020 --> 00:21:22,486
from Transport Canada, though it sailed
from Saint John's with Canadian ships.

351
00:21:22,650 --> 00:21:25,867
The Coast Guard board
also wrote this, about Mr.

352
00:21:26,061 --> 00:21:29,572
Rush, who was both the company's
chief executive and its pilot.

353
00:21:29,882 --> 00:21:33,109
Had he survived the incident, the
board would have recommended that the

354
00:21:33,149 --> 00:21:36,717
Commandant refer the matter to
the Department of Justice, for its

355
00:21:36,757 --> 00:21:40,765
consideration on whether to pursue
a separate criminal investigation.

356
00:21:40,997 --> 00:21:45,106
Whether any crime was committed,
the board wrote, was for the Justice

357
00:21:45,146 --> 00:21:48,573
Department to decide, and that
will not happen, because Mr.

358
00:21:49,209 --> 00:21:50,319
Rush died.

359
00:21:50,930 --> 00:21:54,065
One more finding concerns the
eighteenth of June itself.

360
00:21:54,349 --> 00:21:58,296
The board found that the company's own
communications plan delayed the first

361
00:21:58,336 --> 00:22:02,723
call to rescuers by more than seven
hours, and that no deep-water rescue

362
00:22:02,763 --> 00:22:04,385
vehicle was standing by.

363
00:22:04,688 --> 00:22:08,855
It also found that the implosion caused
the immediate loss of all lives on

364
00:22:08,895 --> 00:22:11,298
board, before any distress call.

365
00:22:11,571 --> 00:22:15,424
The NTSB found that search and
rescue could not have saved them.

366
00:22:15,670 --> 00:22:20,106
With assets ready, it found, Titan
would likely have been found sooner.

367
00:22:20,633 --> 00:22:24,535
OceanGate suspended its
operations in July 2023.

368
00:22:24,893 --> 00:22:28,858
When the Coast Guard report came out,
a company spokesperson said OceanGate

369
00:22:28,898 --> 00:22:33,242
had been wound down, was fully cooperating
with the investigation, and offered

370
00:22:33,282 --> 00:22:35,024
condolences to the families.

371
00:22:35,728 --> 00:22:36,728
What now?

372
00:22:36,818 --> 00:22:41,306
The NTSB made four recommendations
to the US Coast Guard.

373
00:22:41,685 --> 00:22:46,111
One: propose that the International
Maritime Organization make its guidance

374
00:22:46,151 --> 00:22:48,073
for submersibles mandatory.

375
00:22:48,399 --> 00:22:51,688
Two: study pressure
vessels that carry people.

376
00:22:52,014 --> 00:22:54,203
Three: write rules for them.

377
00:22:54,599 --> 00:23:00,109
Four: bring its 1993 guidance up to
date with the law passed that same year.

378
00:23:00,163 --> 00:23:04,970
The Coast Guard agreed to update its
guidance, and partly agreed with the rest.

379
00:23:05,371 --> 00:23:10,245
As of October 2026, all four
recommendations remain open.

380
00:23:10,892 --> 00:23:16,041
On the fifteenth of July, 2022,
Titan's hull changed, and its own

381
00:23:16,081 --> 00:23:17,523
sensors recorded it.

382
00:23:17,905 --> 00:23:22,994
Eleven months later, on its first deep
dive since, five people were aboard.

383
00:23:24,396 --> 00:23:25,529
Stockton Rush.

384
00:23:26,189 --> 00:23:27,867
Paul-Henri Nargeolet.

385
00:23:28,507 --> 00:23:29,819
Shahzada Dawood.

386
00:23:30,449 --> 00:23:31,681
Suleman Dawood.

387
00:23:32,273 --> 00:23:33,425
Hamish Harding.
